### What is the Group Anagrams problem?

Given a list of strings, group them so that all anagrams are in the same group. Two strings are anagrams if one is a rearrangement of the other. For example, `["eat","tea","tan","ate","nat","bat"]` groups into `[["eat","tea","ate"],["tan","nat"],["bat"]]`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class GroupAnagrams {
    public static List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {
        Map<String, List<String>> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (String s : strs) {
            char[] chars = s.toCharArray();
            Arrays.sort(chars);
            String key = new String(chars);
            map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(s);
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(map.values());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] input = {"eat", "tea", "tan", "ate", "nat", "bat"};
        System.out.println(groupAnagrams(input));
    }
}
```

```text
[[eat, tea, ate], [bat], [tan, nat]]
```

### How does the sorted-key approach work?

The core idea is a canonical form. Two strings are anagrams exactly when their sorted character sequences are identical. You convert each string to a char array, sort it, and build a String from the sorted array. This sorted string is the canonical key that is the same for every anagram and different for non-anagrams.

You use this key to bucket strings into a `HashMap<String, List<String>>`. The `computeIfAbsent` method creates an empty list if the key is new, and you add the original string to the list.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class SortedKeyExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] words = {"debit", "bited", "credit"};
        for (String w : words) {
            char[] c = w.toCharArray();
            Arrays.sort(c);
            System.out.println(w + " -> key: " + new String(c));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
debit -> key: bdeit
bited -> key: bdeit
credit -> key: cdeirt
```

### What is the count-signature alternative?

Instead of sorting, build a key string from the 26 letter frequencies. For each word, count how many of each letter it has and join the counts with a separator. This key is O(k) to build instead of O(k log k) for sorting, which is faster for long words over a small alphabet.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class CountSignatureKey {
    public static List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {
        Map<String, List<String>> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (String s : strs) {
            int[] count = new int[26];
            for (char c : s.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
            StringBuilder keyBuilder = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
                keyBuilder.append(count[i]).append('#');
            }
            String key = keyBuilder.toString();
            map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(s);
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(map.values());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] input = {"eat", "tea", "tan", "ate", "nat", "bat"};
        System.out.println(groupAnagrams(input));
    }
}
```

```text
[[eat, tea, ate], [bat], [tan, nat]]
```

### How do the two keying strategies compare?

| Feature | Sorted key | Count-signature key |
|---|---|---|
| Time per word | O(k log k) | O(k) |
| Key length | k characters | Up to 26 counts + separators |
| Collision-free | Yes | Yes |
| Simplicity | Very simple | Slightly more code |
| Best for | Short words, simplicity | Long words, small alphabet |

Both are collision-free canonical forms, meaning two different anagram families never share a key. This is essential for correctness.

### Why is a weak key like char-code sum wrong?

A tempting shortcut is to sum the character codes as a key. This is not collision-free: different letter sets can produce the same sum. For example, `"ad"` (97+100=197) and `"bc"` (98+99=197) have the same sum but are not anagrams.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WeakKeyBug {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Same sum, not anagrams
        String a = "ad", b = "bc";
        int sumA = a.chars().sum();
        int sumB = b.chars().sum();
        System.out.println("Sum of 'ad': " + sumA);
        System.out.println("Sum of 'bc': " + sumB);
        System.out.println("Same sum: " + (sumA == sumB)); // true, but NOT anagrams
    }
}
```

```text
Sum of 'ad': 197
Sum of 'bc': 197
Same sum: true
```

### What about case-insensitive grouping?

If grouping should ignore case, lowercase each string before computing the key. If spaces and punctuation should be ignored, strip them first.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class CaseInsensitiveGrouping {
    public static List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {
        Map<String, List<String>> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (String s : strs) {
            char[] chars = s.toLowerCase().toCharArray();
            Arrays.sort(chars);
            String key = new String(chars);
            map.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(s);
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(map.values());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] tags = {"Debit", "BITED", "Credit"};
        System.out.println(groupAnagrams(tags));
    }
}
```

```text
[[Debit, BITED], [Credit]]
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings (all empty strings are anagrams of each other), single-character strings, a list with one element, and strings of all identical characters.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;

public class GroupAnagramsEdgeCases {
    public static List<List<String>> groupAnagrams(String[] strs) {
        Map<String, List<String>> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (String s : strs) {
            char[] c = s.toCharArray(); Arrays.sort(c);
            map.computeIfAbsent(new String(c), k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(s);
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(map.values());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty strings: " + groupAnagrams(new String[]{"", ""}));
        System.out.println("Single: " + groupAnagrams(new String[]{"a"}));
        System.out.println("All same: " + groupAnagrams(new String[]{"aaa", "aaa"}));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty strings: [[, ]]
Single: [[a]]
All same: [[aaa, aaa]]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

With sorted keys, each word of length k costs O(k log k) to sort. For n words with average length k, the total is O(n * k log k). With count-signature keys, each word costs O(k), giving O(n * k) total. Space is O(n * k) for storing all strings in the map.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Rajesh groups free-form transaction tags into anagram families so Priya can examine one canonical family instead of many shuffled variants. The system uses case-insensitive keying and a `LinkedHashMap` for a stable, first-seen group order in the review report.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class TransactionTagGrouper {
    public static Map<String, List<String>> groupTags(String[] tags) {
        Map<String, List<String>> groups = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (String tag : tags) {
            char[] c = tag.toLowerCase().toCharArray();
            Arrays.sort(c);
            groups.computeIfAbsent(new String(c), k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(tag);
        }
        return groups;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] tags = {"Transfer", "Deposit", "Reftsanr", "Deposti"};
        Map<String, List<String>> grouped = groupTags(tags);
        grouped.forEach((key, group) -> System.out.println("Group: " + group));
    }
}
```

```text
Group: [Transfer, Reftsanr]
Group: [Deposit, Deposti]
```
